# Retired seed paths — instance CONTENT the template no longer ships.
#
# One `<path>\t<reason>` per line, relative to an instance root. Blank lines and
# `#` comments are ignored. Absence of this file, or an empty one, means nothing
# is retired — never an error.
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR, and why it is not a delete list.
#
# Retiring MACHINERY is unambiguous, and since the plugin migration it usually needs
# nothing at all: the plugin tree is replaced whole on every update, so a file this
# repo stops shipping simply stops existing on the machine. A bundle stamped under the
# old design carries symlinks into a template checkout, and /loopd:init's
# conversion sweep removes those, because such a link has exactly one possible meaning.
# Retiring SEED content is not like that. A seed file was copied into the bundle once
# and has been the human's to edit ever since, so its contents may be theirs and theirs
# only — `todos.md` is literally a notes file. The stamp's safety property is that it
# only seeds-if-absent; it never removes bundle content, which is what makes it safe to
# run blindly on a repo full of someone's work. Spending that to save one `rm` would be
# a bad trade.
#
# So: every entry here is REPORTED, with the exact command to remove it, and
# `/loopd:welcome fix` puts it in the numbered "what's left for you" list. Nothing here is
# ever deleted by either script. The decision stays with the human who wrote the
# file.
#
# Add an entry in the same commit that removes the seed file. Leave it here
# permanently — an instance stamped years ago still has the file, and a manifest
# that gets pruned stops answering for exactly those instances.
#
# AN EMPTY ANSWER IS STILL AN ANSWER, so the audit is recorded rather than left to
# be re-derived. The AI Bridge 2.0 plugin migration retired SIXTEEN paths — every
# `symlink/.claude/commands/*.md` file, as each command became a plugin skill, and
# then every `symlink/.claude/agents/*.md` file at the name swap, as the eight role
# agents became the plugin's only copies — and not one of them is seed content. All
# sixteen are MACHINERY, so all sixteen are swept by /loopd:init's conversion and
# none of them belongs below. Verified end to end in tests/retire-machinery.test.sh ("the
# eight commands the plugin absorbed" and "the eight role agents the plugin absorbed"),
# and `git log --diff-filter=D -- seed/` over the same range is empty. The next person
# to retire something reads this and asks which of the two classes it is in.

